% holding-published: daisyfield-japanese-1-sakura
% holding-blocked: Name the book Daisyfield took this koto version from (its page gives none), and check the ending (G A B flat | e d B flat A), which differs from the school version.
% dossier-function: song for the koto: cherry blossom in spring; taught in Japanese schools since the Meiji era
% dossier-elements: in (miyako-bushi) scale on A (A B flat D E F), here a fifth below the usual school key; 4/4; the band holds an open fifth A and E, quietly, as a koto's open strings might
% dossier-practice: slow and even; a koto plays the melody with plucked ornaments; no percussion (the band is kept at low energy)
% dossier-honesty: arranged: the koto melody as Daisyfield transcribed it, words in romaji; the drone is ours
% dossier-licence: public domain (traditional, Edo period; transcription donated to the public domain by Daisyfield.com)
% dossier-respect: for everyone
% dossier-review: a Japanese culture bearer or a teacher of min'yo or koto before publishing
% dossier-accompaniment: Tradition: the koto alone, or voice with koto; no percussion (conservative guess, 11 November 2026). Band here: the minyo style at low energy, an A-E drone like the koto's open strings.
% dossier-lyrics: The old text (Sakura sakura, yayoi no sora wa...) in romaji under the notes, English meaning in an N: line; fit for class; not yet checked by a Japanese speaker (conservative guess, 11 November 2026).
% source-url: https://daisyfield.com/music/jpm/pdf/NGS04-Sakura-Koto.pdf Daisyfield transcription (PDF)
